## Lore

The Tactical Response Group is the special weapons and tactics arm of the
City police, the Australian equivalent of SWAT. Operatives train for four
years, are kept on call, and are sent in when an Administrator decides a
situation is beyond the ordinary police: armed robbery, City Raiders, a
mutant on a rampage, an invasion threat. Beat cops resent the way they take
over, though both answer to the same commanders. Much of a TRG day is
waiting, drilling and paperwork, so field work is prized: investigation,
surveillance, hunting terrorists, smugglers, rogue psychics and organized
crime, and small squad tactics from surgical strikes to guerilla warfare.

To keep its edge the TRG runs what the Cities call Exterior Pacification:
squads ride into neighboring Outback settlements, sometimes undercover and
often not, and intimidate, beat and interrogate the locals to stop crime
and insurrection before it reaches the City. D-Bees, Aboriginals, traders,
adventurers and suspected criminals are the usual targets, and monsters,
practitioners of magic and wanted criminals may be killed on the spot. It
pacifies ordinary Outbackers and breeds hatred in everyone else.

A typical wasteland squad is three to five troopers in medium to heavy
armor on hovercycles or with jet packs, and three to five in power armor.

## GM Notes

Special Weapons Training: the Heavy Weapons choice also gives one extra
attack per melee round when using heavy weapons; the sheet does not add it.
The Power Armor choice covers three different power armor suits chosen by
the player, and that operative has a suit available at all times as his
armor of choice.

Later skills: one additional W.P. at levels 2, 4, 8 and 12, and one
additional skill from Espionage, Military or Technical only at levels 2, 5,
9 and 13. The sheet offers the picks at those levels but does not enforce
which category each comes from.

Authority is the same as the City Police: in uniform, slightly above a
Level 4 citizen.

Issue the sheet does not list: badge and identification. Virtually any
weapon is available when a situation calls for it (explosives, S.D.C. guns,
tazer, M.D. weapons, sniper rifle, rail guns and other heavy weapons),
stored in assault vehicles or assigned as needed. Vehicles that can be
issued: a police patrol cruiser (300 M.D.C., 180 mph/288 km), jet pack (50
M.D.C., 200 mph/320 km, 3000 ft/914 m ceiling), hovercycle (160 M.D.C., 250
mph/400 km), a light assault APC (360 M.D.C., 100 mph/160 km, two gun ports
and a top turret, 2 hours of independent oxygen), or the Security Commando
powered armor suit. Permits for non-issue weapons are easily revoked.

Pay and perks: $3,000 a month, rising $400 per level if conduct is good;
40% off rent and city taxes (or a precinct dormitory), 20% off food and 70%
off medical expenses. Savings are 1D6x1000 dollars. Australian dollars
equal credits.

Rank: starts as a Corporal, with the same ranks as the military. Sergeant,
lieutenant, lieutenant commander and captain are the most common.

One TRG operative in eight is a major or master psychic. Among NPCs, 35%
have 1-4 cybernetics and 20% partial bionics; player characters start with
none.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'police-trg');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'police-trg';

